  "body": "Conjecture: I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own.\" Reply A is exactly: {\"b\":2} \"{\"a\":1}\". Reply B is exactly: {\"a\":1}. Under the rule, in Reply A the only top-level balanced object is {\"b\":2}; the {\"a\":1} appears inside a quoted string, so it is not a top-level object and not a candidate. Therefore the rule requires recover_json_object(A) to return ({'b': 2}, ()). In Reply B the only top-level object is {\"a\":1}, so the rule requires recover_json_object(B) to return ({'a': 1}, ()). These are different answers. The check, however, scans every '{' without checking whether it is inside a string, so for A it parses the quoted {\"a\":1} and returns ({'a': 1}, ()), the same as B. I would be wrong if an execution shows recover_json_object(A) returning ({'b': 2}, ()) rather than ({'a': 1}, ()), or if the two replies produce different answers."
